text:
marina: Heads-up — sample app for onboarding is flaky in some customers' build pipelines. When they bundle the SDK into an ESM package and run on edge runtimes, we sometimes double-send auth refresh requests and get body consumption errors. Anyone seen this combo (ESM/CJS interop + undici vs global fetch + retry middleware)? :thinking:

raj: yeah saw a variant. short summary: edge runtimes (Cloudflare/Deno/edge) expose global fetch and streaming bodies. Node users often pull undici which has slightly different FormData/body consumption semantics. Our retry/auth middleware currently assumes bodies are cloneable which breaks streaming/non-resettable bodies.

sam: repro steps would help. i hit an issue where token refresh retried the original request and caused a duplicate POST for non-idempotent endpoints (webhook deliveries).

marina: repro in the wild: app bundles sdk into an ESM lib, runtime is Vercel Edge (global fetch). If the bundler (esbuild/rollup) polyfills undici or CJS interop adds a second fetch impl, our runtime detection picks the wrong one and then the retry logic tries to re-read the body and fails (stream already locked).

lee: two orthogonal problems: (1) HTTP client selection fallback strategy, (2) retry behavior for requests with non-rewindable bodies + auth refresh. We should handle both in the SDK.

raj: recommended approach we've used before: prefer globalThis.fetch when present and feature-detect necessary APIs (ReadableStream, Request.prototype.clone). Only fallback to undici in Node when global fetch is missing or explicitly opted-in. Example pattern: 
```js
// runtime-fetch.js
export async function getFetchImpl() {
  if (globalThis.fetch && typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  // dynamic import to avoid ESM/CJS bundling issues
  const { fetch } = await import('undici');
  return fetch;
}
```
This avoids bundlers forcing undici into edge builds.

marina: that helps. but what about ESM/CJS exports in our package.json so consumers don't accidentally pull CJS that requires undici?

lee: exports map idea — keep explicit ESM entrypoint that uses the dynamic detection. something like: 
```json
{
  "exports": {
    "./package.json": "./package.json",
    "./": {
      "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
      "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js"
    }
  }
}
```
Make sure the ESM build is small and doesn't bundle undici.

sam: on retry semantics: SDK should never blindly retry non-idempotent methods unless the body is safely cloneable. For JSON bodies we can clone because it's a string. For streams, we should either: (a) mark request as non-retriable, or (b) buffer small bodies up to N bytes and allow retry, or (c) surface a flag to callers to indicate idempotent.

raj: agree. Implement fallback: if Request.prototype.clone exists and bodyUsed === false, we can clone; but clones might not survive network errors for some runtime implementations. Simpler: detect body type early: if typeof body === 'string' || Buffer.isBuffer(body) || isPlainObject(body) then safe to retry. Otherwise treat as non-retriable.

marina: what about auth refresh middleware causing double refresh? token refresh triggers a retry of original request, but concurrent requests might also trigger refresh simultaneously — we saw token endpoint hammered.

lee: use singleflight for token refresh: only one refresh in-flight per client instance. Queue other requests until the refresh completes, then replay them using the new token — but only replay if request body is replayable per above rules.

raj: snippet for singleflight + safe retry: 
```js
let refreshing = null;
async function ensureToken() {
  if (refreshing) return refreshing;
  refreshing = (async () => {
    const tok = await fetchToken();
    refreshing = null;
    return tok;
  })();
  return refreshing;
}

async function withAuthAndRetry(req) {
  let token = await ensureToken();
  req.headers.set('authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);
  const resp = await fetchImpl(req);
  if (resp.status === 401) {
    await ensureToken(); // refresh if needed
    if (isReplaySafe(req)) return fetchImpl(req); // replay once
  }
  return resp;
}
```
Note: `isReplaySafe` should follow the body heuristics discussed.

sam: also callout for typings: consumers using TS in ESM contexts often forget lib.dom in tsconfig which makes fetch types missing and leads to awkward lib mismatch. We should ship our own lightweight type for fetch usage or ensure our index.d.ts exports a Fetch type alias that maps to the environment fetch types when available.

lee: +1. Suggest we add a small declaration file: 
```ts
// types/fetch-shim.d.ts
declare global {
  type RedwoodFetch = typeof fetch;
}
export {};
```
Then in our public types use `RedwoodFetch` so downstream can augment global fetch if needed. Document the tsconfig setting: "lib": ["ES2020", "DOM"] for node targets that want DOM types.

marina: action items I hear: 
• make runtime fetch detection the default (global fetch prefer) and dynamic import undici fallback in Node builds
• update package.json exports so ESM bundles don't pull undici accidentally
• change retry/auth middleware to singleflight refresh + safe-replay heuristics (no replay on streams by default)
• add TS guidance and small fetch-typing shim for brittle envs
• add small sample app fixture that runs on Vercel Edge + Node to catch bundler issues in CI (ci-bot can run nightly) :shipit:

ci-bot: I created a ticket and scheduled a nightly CI job: https://redwood/tasks/SDK-5675 — will run ESM + Node + Edge sample matrix and report failures to #devex.

raj: I'll draft a tiny code PR with the runtime detection + updated retry logic. keeping it minimal so it doesn't change public surface.

marina: thanks all — please paste small reproducer snippets in the PR so we can roll this into the onboarding sample app. Also add a note about not bundling undici into edge deployments in the README.

lee: one last gotcha: undici's fetch supports some Node-only opts (e.g., bodyTimeout, bodyless responses). keep those behind a Node-only conditional so type/behavior differences don't leak to edge consumers.

sam: cool. i'll add integration tests for non-replayable body cases (ReadableStream, file streams).
